[section, $md:
    # Channels and readiness

    A typed channel lets one task send a value to another. A function invocation owns the child tasks it starts and joins them before it returns. These examples describe the accepted Channels V1 design. The compiler and runtime still need to implement it.

    ## Send and receive

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        produce |writer ChannelWriter of String| -> Error! {
            "Priya" >> writer
            "Rob" >> writer
        }

        run || -> Error! {
            reader, writer Channel of String = Channel()
            >> produce(writer)
            writer.close()

            loop << reader is |name| {
                io.line(name)
            }
        }

        run()!
    ]

    `Channel()` creates an unbuffered channel and returns the reader first and the writer second. Prefix `>>` dispatches a direct function or receiver-method call as a child task. Its target has zero ordinary success results. An ordinary call runs on the current task and may suspend.

    In `produce`, infix `>>` sends each String to the writer. Each send waits for a matching receive. Prefix `<<` receives an optional value. The loop binds each present name and prints it. After the last writer closes and the buffer drains, a receive yields `none` and the loop ends.

    A reader parameter uses `ChannelReader of T` and a writer uses `ChannelWriter of T`. Passing an endpoint establishes a scoped obligation for that invocation. Calling `close()` releases that obligation. Other invocations can still use their own endpoints.

    The parent closes its own writer before draining the channel. The child holds its own writer until it exits. Keeping the parent's writer open would leave the parent waiting for another name. Before returning, `run` joins the child. A child failure reaches the caller through its owning invocation.
]

[section, $md:
    ## Choose a ready operation

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        if {
            case incoming = << reader if listening:
                handle(incoming)
            case pending >> writer:
                io.line("sent")
            else:
                io.line("nothing ready")
        }
    ]

    Each `case` offers one send or receive. The optional Boolean guard enables the operation. Here the receive guard can use `listening`. The prospective binding `incoming` first exists in the winning body, where `handle` receives the optional result. Write `case << reader:` to discard the received value.

    The scheduler selects fairly among ready operations and commits at most one. A losing receive consumes nothing and a losing send retains its payload. The final `else:` runs immediately when no enabled operation is ready. Omit it to wait. For an unconditional operation, write the send or receive directly.

    Once a reader reaches end of stream it stays ready and returns `none`. A repeated selection needs to handle closure or disable that arm. A send with no readers is also ready. If selected, it raises a recoverable error, skips its success body and leaves responsibility for the unaccepted payload with the sender.
]

[section, $md:
    ## Buffering and task completion

    [codeblock, $code "moth":
        reader, writer Channel of String = Channel(capacity = 32)
    ]

    This channel can queue up to 32 strings. A full queue makes a sender wait. An empty, open queue makes a reader wait. Several readers and writers can share the channel. Each accepted message reaches one reader and queued messages come out in order.

    Dispatch evaluates the written arguments once in source order. If preparation fails, the child never starts. A child can return a built-in error slot. When a child fails, its owning invocation cancels its other children, waits for cleanup and delivers the failure. Handle it at the owning call. If the parent fails first, it cancels and joins its children before delivering its own error.

    A task can give others a turn with `io.task.yield()`. To request cancellation of its own outstanding children, an invocation calls `io.task.cancel_children()`. It still joins them before exiting. Cancelling a task can withdraw a pending channel operation. A send that already committed keeps its effect.
]

[section, $md:
    ## Value and task lifetimes

    A successful send transfers cleanup responsibility for an independent value to the channel. Receiving transfers that responsibility to the receiver. An independently transferable graph must already satisfy the bounded channel or task lifetime. A failed or losing send leaves its value with the sender.

    Storage placed with `into` belongs to its declared region until bulk cleanup. To send data from such a region, construct a fresh transferable value or an eligible independent `copy`. Closing a region reclaims its storage. The function invocation owns the child join.

    The accepted memory contract uses cooperative, single-threaded scheduling and non-atomic retained-edge counts. @../../developer-docs/memory-management (Memory management) describes the underlying lifetime rules. See @../progress (Progress) for implementation status.
]
